{
  "id": "chunk_13",
  "chunk": "that humans acquire naturally through interaction with the physical world.\nThe question of whether LLMs possess genuine understanding remains open\nfor debate. Bender and Koller [3] argue that language models trained only on\nlinguistic patterns cannot acquire meaning without grounding in the real world.\nMarcus and Davis [20] similarly contend that deep learning systems lack the\nrobust, compositional understanding characteristic of human cognition. While\nsome researchers argue that sufficiently large models may develop emergent\nunderstanding [5], critics maintain that pattern matching—however sophisti-\ncated—differs fundamentally from comprehension [24].\nAlso Mitchell warns against the danger of “wishful mnemonics” [21]—using\nterms like “understands,” “learns,” or “thinks” to describe AI systems in ways\nthat unconsciously shape our perception of their capabilities and lead us to\noverestimate their intelligence.\n2.2 Metacognition in LLMs",
